  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(4,7)$, expressed in standard form?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{7-0}{4-0}=\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(0,0).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{7}{4}(x-0) \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{7}{4}(x) \\\\ 4\\cdot (y) & =4\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}(x) \\\\ 4y & =7x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y & =7x \\\\ -7x+4y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+4y & =0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+4y) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 7x-4y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $7x-4y=0.$",
